00:02:45.959 --> 00:02:48.839
A story: the trending shelf that only sold milk.

40
00:02:49.119 --> 00:03:00.852
The first Trending near you shelf ran SELECT sku, count() FROM views WHERE ts now() - interval '1 hour' GROUP BY sku ORDER BY 2 DESC LIMIT 10 on the analytics replica every minute.

41
00:03:01.132 --> 00:03:02.753
It went wrong in several ways:

42
00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:06.532
It always showed milk, bread, onions, tomatoes and eggs.

43
00:03:06.812 --> 00:03:08.604
They were popular, not trending.

44
00:03:08.884 --> 00:03:12.852
The mango-festival spike a marketing team had paid for never appeared.

45
00:03:13.132 --> 00:03:14.561
The query slowed down.

46
00:03:14.841 --> 00:03:19.748
At 50k views per second, the hourly GROUP BY scanned 180M rows.

47
00:03:20.028 --> 00:03:24.167
It took 40 seconds, then 3 minutes, and then the replica fell behind.

48
00:03:25.517 --> 00:03:31.746
The rewrite split counting across 16 stream partitions, each keeping its own top-10, and merged them.

49
00:03:32.026 --> 00:03:35.013
Items popular everywhere but top nowhere vanished.

50
00:03:35.293 --> 00:03:38.087
Finance noticed the numbers didn't match the warehouse.

51
00:03:38.367 --> 00:03:43.701
Daily active customers on the ops dashboard was the sum of hourly store-level uniques.

52
00:03:43.981 --> 00:03:48.503
It said 6 million on a day the platform had 1.5 million customers.

53
00:03:48.783 --> 00:03:51.322
The fix for (2) switched to growth ratios.

54
00:03:51.602 --> 00:03:54.738
The shelf filled with items that went from 1 view to 4.

