00:16:18.344 --> 00:16:19.965
Popularity is not trend.

319
00:16:20.245 --> 00:16:23.296
The most-viewed list is the same staples every hour.

320
00:16:23.576 --> 00:16:28.803
Growth ratios reward noise in the long tail (0, then 3 is 4 times ).

321
00:16:29.083 --> 00:16:36.592
The surprise score compares an item with its own expected level for this hour of day, scaled by Poisson noise (√μ).

322
00:16:36.872 --> 00:16:39.603
It finds real surges anywhere in the catalogue.

323
00:16:39.883 --> 00:16:42.656
A floor and a threshold make the shelf trustworthy.

324
00:16:42.936 --> 00:16:50.189
Almost all the misses were SKUs expecting under 2 views or hour, which is too small to separate from noise within hours.

325
00:16:50.469 --> 00:16:54.885
A shelf that shows fewer than 10 items is fine; a shelf of noise is not.

326
00:16:56.235 --> 00:16:57.473
Production additions:

327
00:16:57.753 --> 00:16:59.267
Decay, so trends fade.

328
00:16:59.547 --> 00:17:00.913
Per-city baselines.

329
00:17:01.193 --> 00:17:06.462
Suppressing items that are out of stock, or not allowed on the shelf (alcohol rules, recalls).

330
00:17:06.742 --> 00:17:09.707
Bot filtering, because fake views create fake trends.

331
00:17:09.987 --> 00:17:11.438
Editorial overrides.

332
00:17:12.788 --> 00:17:14.153
The streaming pipeline.

333
00:17:14.433 --> 00:17:22.305
Ingest: apps, then collectors, then Kafka (keyed by the aggregation key where possible, for example, city:sku for top-K).

334
00:17:22.585 --> 00:17:25.103
Schema-managed events (Ch 28).

335
00:17:25.383 --> 00:17:26.343
Process:

336
00:17:26.623 --> 00:17:32.809
Flink or Kafka Streams with event-time windows, watermarks and allowed lateness (Ch 21).

337
00:17:33.089 --> 00:17:35.521
Keyed state holds sketches per key and bucket.

338
00:17:35.801 --> 00:17:37.807
Checkpoints give exactly-once state.

339
00:17:38.087 --> 00:17:38.812
Serve:

340
00:17:39.092 --> 00:17:43.337
App-facing shelves are precomputed into Redis (a sorted set per city).

341
00:17:44.687 --> 00:17:50.682
Dashboards use an OLAP store (Pinot or Druid or ClickHouse) with rollups and sketch columns.

342
00:17:50.962 --> 00:17:53.863
Nothing app-facing runs ad-hoc aggregation.

343
00:17:54.143 --> 00:18:01.034
Lambda versus Kappa: streaming gives fresh approximate numbers; batch (the warehouse) gives the reconciled truth.

344
00:18:01.314 --> 00:18:05.986
Many teams run both, and label the dashboards live (approx.) versus final.

345
00:18:07.336 --> 00:18:09.149
Exactly-once counts for billing.

346
00:18:09.429 --> 00:18:13.141
Sponsored-listing clicks are billed to brands, so they must be exact:

347
00:18:13.421 --> 00:18:23.619
Dedup by click id (the client generates a UUID; the server signs impression ids) in a keyed state with a TTL longer than the maximum lateness.

348
00:18:23.899 --> 00:18:28.080
Filter bots and fraud (rate, device and pattern checks) before counting.

349
00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:29.491
Publish the rules.

350
00:18:29.771 --> 00:18:36.960
Exactly-once sinks: transactional writes (Kafka transactions, idempotent upserts keyed by window and campaign).

351
00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:45.731
Reconcile daily against the batch recomputation from raw logs, with any difference explained (Ch 45 reconciliation style).

352
00:18:47.081 --> 00:18:48.211
Interview framing.

353
00:18:48.491 --> 00:18:54.166
Design a system to show the top-K most viewed products or trending items or count ad clicks.

354
00:18:54.446 --> 00:18:59.865
Clarify: window;. exact versus approximate;. freshness;. per-dimension breakdowns.

355
00:19:00.145 --> 00:19:02.150
Then cover: partitioning by key;.

356
00:19:03.500 --> 00:19:12.652
sketches (Count-Min, Space-Saving, HLL) and their errors;. window mechanics;. precomputed serving;. merge correctness;. reconciliation.

357
00:19:12.932 --> 00:19:21.465
Staff-level signals: saying local top-K doesn't merge and distinct counts don't add, and defining trending as a baseline anomaly.

