549
00:35:51.577 --> 00:35:52.515
Part 6.

550
00:35:52.795 --> 00:35:54.075
Production Concerns.

551
00:35:55.325 --> 00:35:56.477
Failure modes.

552
00:35:56.757 --> 00:35:58.101
Here is a quick table:

553
00:35:58.381 --> 00:36:00.664
Failure, and Symptom, and Mitigation.

554
00:36:00.944 --> 00:36:02.352
GROUP BY on raw events.

555
00:36:02.632 --> 00:36:03.443
Symptom:

556
00:36:03.723 --> 00:36:05.131
Slow, replica lag.

557
00:36:05.411 --> 00:36:06.413
Mitigation:

558
00:36:06.693 --> 00:36:09.189
Stream pre-aggregation, OLAP rollups.

559
00:36:09.469 --> 00:36:11.837
Exact counters for huge key spaces.

560
00:36:12.117 --> 00:36:12.928
Symptom:

561
00:36:13.208 --> 00:36:14.851
Memory blow-up (L1).

562
00:36:15.131 --> 00:36:16.133
Mitigation:

563
00:36:16.413 --> 00:36:17.949
Sketches, bounded state.

564
00:36:18.229 --> 00:36:19.851
Merging local top-K.

565
00:36:20.131 --> 00:36:20.941
Symptom:

566
00:36:21.221 --> 00:36:23.483
Missing items, wrong counts (L2).

567
00:36:23.763 --> 00:36:24.765
Mitigation:

568
00:36:25.045 --> 00:36:26.859
Partition by key, TPUT.

569
00:36:27.139 --> 00:36:28.525
Summing distinct counts.

570
00:36:28.805 --> 00:36:29.616
Symptom:

571
00:36:29.896 --> 00:36:32.115
Wildly inflated uniques (L3).

572
00:36:32.395 --> 00:36:33.397
Mitigation:

573
00:36:33.677 --> 00:36:35.491
HLL or Theta merges.

574
00:36:35.771 --> 00:36:37.307
Popularity as trending.

575
00:36:37.587 --> 00:36:38.397
Symptom:

576
00:36:38.677 --> 00:36:40.597
Staples every hour (L4).

577
00:36:40.877 --> 00:36:41.880
Mitigation:

578
00:36:42.160 --> 00:36:43.675
Baseline anomaly score.

579
00:36:43.955 --> 00:36:45.043
Growth ratios.

580
00:36:45.323 --> 00:36:46.133
Symptom:

581
00:36:46.413 --> 00:36:47.779
Tail noise (L4).

582
00:36:48.059 --> 00:36:49.061
Mitigation:

583
00:36:49.341 --> 00:36:51.517
Poisson-scaled z, volume floor.

584
00:36:52.867 --> 00:36:53.870
Late events.

585
00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:54.961
Symptom:

586
00:36:55.241 --> 00:36:56.158
Under-counts.

587
00:36:56.438 --> 00:36:57.441
Mitigation:

588
00:36:57.721 --> 00:37:00.174
Watermarks, allowed lateness, revisions.

589
00:37:00.454 --> 00:37:01.286
Bots.

590
00:37:01.566 --> 00:37:02.377
Symptom:

591
00:37:02.657 --> 00:37:04.534
Fake trends, inflated clicks.

592
00:37:04.814 --> 00:37:05.817
Mitigation:

593
00:37:06.097 --> 00:37:07.675
Filtering before counting.

594
00:37:07.955 --> 00:37:09.705
Stream versus batch mismatch.

595
00:37:09.985 --> 00:37:10.795
Symptom:

596
00:37:11.075 --> 00:37:11.993
Distrust.

597
00:37:12.273 --> 00:37:13.275
Mitigation:

598
00:37:13.555 --> 00:37:15.326
Reconciliation, labels.

599
00:37:16.676 --> 00:37:17.764
Observability.

600
00:37:18.044 --> 00:37:21.927
Consumer lag per partition, watermark delay, late-event rate.

601
00:37:22.207 --> 00:37:27.241
Sketch error checks: periodic exact counts on samples versus sketch estimates.

602
00:37:27.521 --> 00:37:31.553
Trending shelf precision from labelled replays; editorial feedback.

603
00:37:31.833 --> 00:37:34.244
Billing reconciliation diffs per campaign.

604
00:37:35.594 --> 00:37:37.151
Security and privacy.

605
00:37:37.431 --> 00:37:42.210
Aggregate before exposing: dashboards show counts, not user-level trails.

606
00:37:42.490 --> 00:37:45.839
HLL registers can leak membership in small sets.

607
00:37:46.119 --> 00:37:52.391
Don't expose raw sketches publicly, and apply minimum-count thresholds (k-anonymity) on breakdowns.

608
00:37:52.671 --> 00:37:55.317
Signed click ids to prevent billing fraud.

609
00:37:56.667 --> 00:37:57.563
Performance.

610
00:37:57.843 --> 00:38:01.363
Key streams by aggregation key to avoid shuffles later.

611
00:38:01.643 --> 00:38:06.379
Pre-aggregate at the edge (collector-side micro-batches) for very hot keys.

612
00:38:06.659 --> 00:38:10.307
Precompute app-facing lists and keep them in Redis sorted sets.

613
00:38:11.657 --> 00:38:12.382
Cost.

614
00:38:12.662 --> 00:38:17.867
Sketches cut state and storage by one to two orders of magnitude (L1, L3).

615
00:38:18.147 --> 00:38:22.457
Keep raw events in cheap storage for batch truth; keep only rollups hot.

616
00:38:22.737 --> 00:38:27.174
Sampling for exploratory dashboards; exact only where money depends on it.

617
00:38:28.524 --> 00:38:29.463
Part 7.

618
00:38:29.743 --> 00:38:31.449
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

619
00:38:32.699 --> 00:38:33.617
Watch out:

620
00:38:33.897 --> 00:38:34.537
1.

621
00:38:34.817 --> 00:38:37.590
Ad-hoc GROUP BY on raw events for live views.

622
00:38:37.870 --> 00:38:39.385
Scans grow with traffic.

623
00:38:40.735 --> 00:38:41.652
Watch out:

624
00:38:41.932 --> 00:38:42.636
2.

625
00:38:42.916 --> 00:38:45.113
Exact counters for every key forever.

626
00:38:45.393 --> 00:38:47.761
State grows with the key space (L1).

627
00:38:49.111 --> 00:38:50.029
Watch out:

628
00:38:50.309 --> 00:38:51.055
3.

629
00:38:51.335 --> 00:38:53.213
Merging local top-K lists.

630
00:38:53.493 --> 00:38:57.354
Items that are high everywhere but top nowhere disappear (L2).

631
00:38:58.704 --> 00:38:59.621
Watch out:

632
00:38:59.901 --> 00:39:00.605
4.

633
00:39:00.885 --> 00:39:02.592
Assuming c, K is enough.

634
00:39:02.872 --> 00:39:05.027
It usually works, with no guarantee.

635
00:39:06.377 --> 00:39:07.294
Watch out:

636
00:39:07.574 --> 00:39:08.342
5.

637
00:39:08.622 --> 00:39:12.867
Summing distinct counts. +316% in L3.

638
00:39:14.217 --> 00:39:15.135
Watch out:

639
00:39:15.415 --> 00:39:16.161
6.

640
00:39:16.441 --> 00:39:18.233
Exact sets for uniques at scale.

641
00:39:18.513 --> 00:39:21.735
Hundreds of MB and seconds-long unions (L3).

642
00:39:23.085 --> 00:39:24.002
Watch out:

643
00:39:24.282 --> 00:39:25.029
7.

644
00:39:25.309 --> 00:39:27.015
HLL for intersections.

645
00:39:27.295 --> 00:39:29.685
Inclusion, exclusion error explodes.

646
00:39:29.965 --> 00:39:31.330
Use Theta sketches.

647
00:39:32.680 --> 00:39:33.597
Watch out:

648
00:39:33.877 --> 00:39:34.560
8.

649
00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:36.461
Trending = most viewed.

650
00:39:36.741 --> 00:39:38.661
Staples every hour (L4).

651
00:39:40.011 --> 00:39:40.929
Watch out:

652
00:39:41.209 --> 00:39:41.955
9.

653
00:39:42.235 --> 00:39:44.027
Trending = growth ratio.

654
00:39:44.307 --> 00:39:45.673
Tail noise (L4).

655
00:39:47.023 --> 00:39:47.940
Watch out:

656
00:39:48.220 --> 00:39:48.924
10.

657
00:39:49.204 --> 00:39:50.591
Always filling the shelf.

658
00:39:50.871 --> 00:39:52.855
Forcing 10 items forces noise.

659
00:39:54.205 --> 00:39:55.122
Watch out:

660
00:39:55.402 --> 00:39:56.234
11.

661
00:39:56.514 --> 00:39:58.157
Approximate counts for billing.

662
00:39:58.437 --> 00:40:02.234
Finance needs exact, deduplicated, reconciled numbers.

663
00:40:03.584 --> 00:40:04.501
Watch out:

664
00:40:04.781 --> 00:40:05.571
12.

665
00:40:05.851 --> 00:40:07.856
Unlabelled approximate dashboards.

666
00:40:08.136 --> 00:40:11.357
People make decisions on numbers they assume are exact.

667
00:40:12.707 --> 00:40:13.582
Part 8.

668
00:40:13.862 --> 00:40:15.206
Interview Q and A.

669
00:40:16.456 --> 00:40:17.288
Beginner.

670
00:40:17.568 --> 00:40:18.592
Question 1.

671
00:40:18.872 --> 00:40:21.901
Design 'top 10 most viewed products in the last hour'.

672
00:40:22.181 --> 00:40:23.547
Thirty-second answer:

673
00:40:23.827 --> 00:40:39.827
Stream view events through Kafka keyed by product (or city:product); a stream processor keeps per-minute counts per key and maintains a top-K per window by merging the last 60 buckets; results are written to a Redis sorted set that the app reads.

674
00:40:40.107 --> 00:40:45.056
For huge key spaces, use Space-Saving or Count-Min + heap to bound memory.

675
00:40:46.406 --> 00:40:47.409
Question 2.

676
00:40:47.689 --> 00:40:49.139
What is a Count-Min sketch?

677
00:40:49.419 --> 00:40:50.785
Thirty-second answer:

678
00:40:51.065 --> 00:41:00.174
A d times w array of counters with d hash functions; each event increments one counter per row; the estimate is the minimum across rows.

679
00:41:00.454 --> 00:41:13.553
It never under-estimates, over-estimates by at most εN with probability 1minus δ (w = e or ε, d = ln 1 or δ), and sketches merge by adding.

680
00:41:14.903 --> 00:41:15.948
Question 3.

681
00:41:16.228 --> 00:41:18.937
Why can't you sum unique-user counts across hours?

682
00:41:19.217 --> 00:41:20.583
Thirty-second answer:

683
00:41:20.863 --> 00:41:25.620
The same user appears in many hours (and stores); summing counts them repeatedly.

684
00:41:25.900 --> 00:41:31.191
Store mergeable sketches (HLL) at the finest grain and union them for any range.

685
00:41:32.541 --> 00:41:33.607
Question 4.

686
00:41:33.887 --> 00:41:35.295
What is HyperLogLog?

687
00:41:35.575 --> 00:41:36.941
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:41:37.221 --> 00:41:47.994
A cardinality estimator that hashes items and tracks, per register, the maximum number of leading zeros seen; the harmonic mean across registers estimates the count.

689
00:41:48.274 --> 00:41:56.637
Fixed small memory ( at most 12 KB in Redis), ~0.8% error, and union by taking register maxima.

690
00:41:57.987 --> 00:41:58.989
Intermediate.

691
00:41:59.269 --> 00:42:00.379
Question 5.

692
00:42:00.659 --> 00:42:03.667
How do you merge top-K results from many partitions?

693
00:42:03.947 --> 00:42:05.312
Thirty-second answer:

694
00:42:05.592 --> 00:42:08.067
Local top-K lists don't merge correctly.

695
00:42:08.347 --> 00:42:24.240
Either partition by the ranking key so each item lives in one partition (then merging disjoint local top-Ks is exact), or use a threshold algorithm like TPUT that fetches all items above a bound, or merge mergeable sketches.

696
00:42:25.590 --> 00:42:26.699
Question 6.

697
00:42:26.979 --> 00:42:29.326
What is Space-Saving (or Misra, Gries)?

698
00:42:29.606 --> 00:42:30.971
Thirty-second answer:

699
00:42:31.251 --> 00:42:39.017
Keep k counters; increment if the item has one; otherwise replace the minimum counter and inherit its count (+1).

700
00:42:39.297 --> 00:42:46.166
Every item with frequency N or k is guaranteed to be present, with over-estimation at most N or k.

701
00:42:47.516 --> 00:42:48.604
Question 7.

702
00:42:48.884 --> 00:42:50.335
How do you define 'trending'?

703
00:42:50.615 --> 00:42:51.980
Thirty-second answer:

704
00:42:52.260 --> 00:43:09.796
Items whose current activity is surprisingly high relative to their own baseline for this time of day and place: a z-score like (x minus μ)/√(μ+1) with a seasonal EWMA baseline, a minimum volume, a significance threshold, confirmation and decay.

705
00:43:11.146 --> 00:43:12.170
Question 8.

706
00:43:12.450 --> 00:43:15.010
How do windows work in streaming aggregation?

707
00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:16.655
Thirty-second answer:

708
00:43:16.935 --> 00:43:31.122
Tumbling (fixed, non-overlapping), sliding or hopping (overlapping), and session windows; computed in event time with watermarks and allowed lateness; sliding windows are usually built from small tumbling buckets merged.

709
00:43:32.472 --> 00:43:33.539
Question 9.

710
00:43:33.819 --> 00:43:35.739
Where should live dashboards query from?

711
00:43:36.019 --> 00:43:37.384
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:43:37.664 --> 00:43:51.104
An OLAP store designed for real-time ingest and aggregation (Pinot, Druid, ClickHouse) with rollups and sketch columns, not the OLTP database or raw logs; app-facing lists precomputed into a cache.

713
00:43:52.454 --> 00:43:53.222
Senior.

714
00:43:53.502 --> 00:43:54.547
Question 10.

715
00:43:54.827 --> 00:43:58.134
Design ad-click aggregation that finance can bill on.

716
00:43:58.414 --> 00:43:59.779
Thirty-second answer:

717
00:44:00.059 --> 00:44:23.121
Signed impression and click ids; collectors, then Kafka; dedup by click id in keyed state with TTL; bot or fraud filtering; windowed exact counts per campaign; exactly-once sink (transactions or idempotent upserts by window and campaign); OLAP for reporting; daily batch recomputation from raw logs and reconciliation with explained diffs.

718
00:44:24.471 --> 00:44:25.601
Question 11.

719
00:44:25.881 --> 00:44:29.337
How do you roll up distinct counts across arbitrary dimensions?

720
00:44:29.617 --> 00:44:30.983
Thirty-second answer:

721
00:44:31.263 --> 00:44:43.892
Store HLL (or Theta) sketches at the finest grain (store times hour times segment); union sketches at query time for any combination; OLAP stores support sketch columns natively.

722
00:44:45.242 --> 00:44:46.330
Question 12.

723
00:44:46.610 --> 00:44:49.682
How do you handle a very hot key in stream aggregation?

724
00:44:49.962 --> 00:44:51.327
Thirty-second answer:

725
00:44:51.607 --> 00:45:05.261
Pre-aggregate before the shuffle (local combiners or micro-batches at collectors), split the key with salting and merge (two-stage aggregation), and give the hot key more parallelism; sketches merge naturally.

726
00:45:06.611 --> 00:45:07.891
Question 13.

727
00:45:08.171 --> 00:45:10.283
How do you verify approximate numbers?

728
00:45:10.563 --> 00:45:11.928
Thirty-second answer:

729
00:45:12.208 --> 00:45:24.048
Compare against exact batch recomputation on samples or daily; monitor error distributions; label dashboards as approximate; set error budgets per metric; alert on drift.

730
00:45:25.398 --> 00:45:26.593
Question 14.

731
00:45:26.873 --> 00:45:29.475
How would you build 'trending near you' per city at scale?

732
00:45:29.755 --> 00:45:31.121
Thirty-second answer:

733
00:45:31.401 --> 00:45:48.873
Key events by city:SKU; per-key minute counts and seasonal baselines in Flink state; z-score with volume floor per minute; confirmation and decay; filter out-of-stock and policy items; write per-city sorted sets to Redis; replay-based evaluation with labelled events.

734
00:45:50.223 --> 00:45:51.119
Architect.

735
00:45:51.399 --> 00:45:52.593
Question 15.

736
00:45:52.873 --> 00:45:56.031
Design the real-time analytics platform end to end.

737
00:45:56.311 --> 00:45:57.676
Thirty-second answer:

738
00:45:57.956 --> 00:46:16.260
Collectors, then Kafka (keyed by aggregation keys), then Flink with event-time windows and sketch state, then Redis for app-facing lists, OLAP for dashboards, exactly-once billing path; raw to the lake; batch warehouse as truth; reconciliation; governance of metric definitions.

739
00:46:17.610 --> 00:46:18.805
Question 16.

740
00:46:19.085 --> 00:46:20.237
Lambda or Kappa?

741
00:46:20.517 --> 00:46:21.882
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:46:22.162 --> 00:46:37.991
Kappa (one streaming codebase, reprocess by replaying Kafka) is simpler when retention allows replay and the stream processor is exact enough; Lambda (stream for fresh, batch for truth) persists where money or audits need batch recomputation.

743
00:46:38.271 --> 00:46:42.666
Many run Kappa for features and Lambda-style reconciliation for billing.

744
00:46:44.016 --> 00:46:45.360
Question 17.

745
00:46:45.640 --> 00:46:49.416
How do you choose between Pinot, Druid, ClickHouse and a warehouse?

746
00:46:49.696 --> 00:46:51.061
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:46:51.341 --> 00:47:07.512
Pinot or Druid excel at real-time ingest with low-latency, high-QPS aggregations (user-facing analytics); ClickHouse at fast analytical SQL with flexible schemas; warehouses (BigQuery, Snowflake) at ad hoc and batch.

748
00:47:07.792 --> 00:47:11.803
Choose by freshness, QPS, query shapes, and operations skills.

749
00:47:13.153 --> 00:47:14.326
Question 18.

750
00:47:14.606 --> 00:47:17.806
How can analytics be manipulated, and how do you defend?

751
00:47:18.086 --> 00:47:19.451
Thirty-second answer:

752
00:47:19.731 --> 00:47:36.371
Bots inflate views to fake trends or clicks to drain competitors' ad budgets; defend with device and behaviour signals, rate limits, signed ids, anomaly detection on traffic sources, excluding flagged traffic before counting, and auditing trending changes.

753
00:47:37.721 --> 00:47:38.959
Question 19.

754
00:47:39.239 --> 00:47:42.439
How do you keep metric definitions consistent across teams?

755
00:47:42.719 --> 00:47:44.084
Thirty-second answer:

756
00:47:44.364 --> 00:48:03.116
A semantic layer or metrics catalogue with owned definitions (for example, active customer = opened app, excluding bots, per IST day), computed once centrally, versioned, documented; dashboards reference metrics, not raw SQL; enforce that distinct counts come from sketches.

757
00:48:04.466 --> 00:48:05.575
Question 20.

758
00:48:05.855 --> 00:48:08.223
What makes real-time analytics trustworthy?

759
00:48:08.503 --> 00:48:09.869
Thirty-second answer:

760
00:48:10.149 --> 00:48:22.927
Correct merges and distinct counts, known and monitored error for approximations, clear labels, reconciliation with batch, controlled definitions, bot filtering, and lag or lateness visibility.

